    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An Edo kingdom in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Obas</a> built a regional empire from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> from the 15th century.</li><li>Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>, among the finest figurative sculpture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>.</li><li>A British expedition burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-city">Benin City</a> in 1897 and looted its art, now at the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a> debate.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Much of the metal for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> came from Portuguese brass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manilla">manillas</a>, horseshoe-shaped bracelets used as currency in trade.</p></aside>
    </section>

